<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n35677" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">pĕr-urbānus</span></b> , a,
		um, adj., <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>very polite</i>, <i>pleasant</i>, or
				<i>witty.</i>
		</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">L. Torquatus elegans in dicendo, toto genere
					perurbanus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cic.
					Brut. 68, 239</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">C. Lucilius et doctus et perurbanus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id.
					de Or. 1, 16, 72</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Transf.</b>, with a bad accessory signif., <i>over-fine</i>, <i>over-polite</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">cum rusticis potius quam cum his perurbanis,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cic.
					Att. 2, 15, 3</span></i>
			. —<i>Adv.</i>: <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">pĕrurbānē</span></b> , <i>very elegantly</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">respondere,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Sid. Ep. 5, 17 <i>fin.</i></span></i>
			</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
